WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.500
The density function looks awkward, but the cumulative function — the probability of scoring below some threshold — comes out far simpler.

2
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:17.525
Written in terms of actual scores rather than normalised ones, it takes this form, with lambda the inverse of the standard deviation.

3
00:00:18.125 --> 00:00:23.425
And for BLAST alignments, the peak of that distribution is given by this expression.

4
00:00:23.425 --> 00:00:26.625
The derivation is in the original paper.

5
00:00:27.225 --> 00:00:33.075
Which brings us to the E value — an estimate of how many false positives a search will return.

6
00:00:33.075 --> 00:00:37.800
It depends on the score, and on lambda, which scales the scoring system.

7
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:44.475
It depends on the length of the query and the size of the database, because both make chance matches more likely.

8
00:00:44.475 --> 00:00:52.300
And it depends on K, which accounts for something easy to miss: alignments starting at different places are not independent.

9
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:59.150
A high-scoring alignment starting at residues one and one implies a decent score starting at two and two as well.

10
00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:04.325
K is usually around nought point one, and it matters less than the other terms.

11
00:01:04.325 --> 00:01:08.300
K and lambda together are the Karlin-Altschul statistics.

12
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:14.375
A BLAST report gives you three numbers: the E value, the raw score, and the bit score.

13
00:01:14.375 --> 00:01:22.150
The raw score comes straight from the substitution matrix and the gap penalties, so it is unitless and means very little on its own.

14
00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:34.525
The bit score is the raw score normalised by the statistics of the scoring system, and that is what makes it useful — bit scores can be compared between searches, even searches that used different matrices.

15
00:01:34.525 --> 00:01:40.625
They also connect the two: if you know the size of the search space, the bit score gives you the E value.

16
00:01:40.625 --> 00:01:44.225
The difference between the two is worth holding on to.

17
00:01:44.225 --> 00:01:48.800
The E value is how many alignments this good you would expect by chance.

18
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:54.950
The bit score is a measure of the alignment itself, independent of how big the database was.

19
00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:59.775
Higher is better, and below about fifty is generally not to be trusted.

20
00:02:00.375 --> 00:02:08.850
The size of the search space is the query length times the database length — every place the query could sit against anything in the database.

21
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.450
BLAST adjusts that slightly.

22
00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:21.300
An alignment of average length cannot start right at the end of a sequence, so the average alignment length is subtracted from both terms, giving the effective search space.

23
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:27.825
Now the second part of the lecture: PSI-BLAST, position-specific iterated BLAST.

24
00:02:27.825 --> 00:02:31.250
The idea is to use what the first search found.

25
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:40.175
Run an ordinary BLAST search, take the high-scoring hits, align them, and build a scoring matrix that is specific to each position of that alignment.

26
00:02:40.175 --> 00:02:43.525
Then search again with that matrix, and repeat.

27
00:02:43.525 --> 00:02:50.000
Everything underneath is ordinary BLAST — the same words, the same extension, the same statistics.

28
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.925
Two things change.

29
00:02:51.925 --> 00:02:59.750
The fixed substitution matrix is replaced by a position-specific one, and the search becomes iterative instead of single-pass.

30
00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:05.225
What you gain is sensitivity to distant relatives, which a single search misses.

31
00:03:05.225 --> 00:03:11.775
What you risk is that one wrong sequence entering the profile pulls in more wrong sequences on the next round.

32
00:03:11.775 --> 00:03:15.200
The iteration is powerful and it needs watching.

33
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.475
So, how is that position-specific matrix built?

34
00:03:19.475 --> 00:03:23.000
Start from a multiple sequence alignment of the hits.

35
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:27.625
At each position, count how often each of the twenty residues occurs.

36
00:03:27.625 --> 00:03:35.500
At the first position you might see six alanines, two lysines, a leucine and a glutamate, in an alignment of ten sequences.

37
00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:42.050
Then add pseudocounts — small amounts added to every residue, not only the ones that came out zero.

38
00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:44.950
We will see shortly why that matters.

39
00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:47.625
Turn the counts into frequencies.

40
00:03:47.625 --> 00:03:58.975
Compare each against the background frequency of that residue, which is simply how often it occurs in proteins generally: around eight per cent for alanine, one and a half for tryptophan.

41
00:03:58.975 --> 00:04:02.950
Finally take the logarithm of that ratio, in base two.

42
00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:11.625
A score of plus one means the residue is twice as likely here as by chance; plus three means eight times; minus one means half as likely.

43
00:04:11.625 --> 00:04:17.250
Base two is conventional, and it keeps the numbers in a range you can read at a glance.

44
00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:24.925
Arrange those scores in a table — positions down, residues across — and that is the position-specific scoring matrix.

45
00:04:25.525 --> 00:04:30.025
Here is a worked example: five sequences, five positions.

46
00:04:30.025 --> 00:04:33.150
First the raw counts, without pseudocounts.

47
00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:38.150
Residues that never occur at all are left out of the table to keep it readable.

48
00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:42.075
Now add a pseudocount of one to every cell.

49
00:04:42.675 --> 00:04:50.975
With the alignment in hand, the first step is simply counting: at each position, how many times each residue appears down the column.

50
00:04:51.575 --> 00:04:54.200
And here is why that matters.

51
00:04:54.200 --> 00:05:03.950
Without pseudocounts, a residue that is naturally rare — and not the dominant residue in the alignment either — can end up dominating the sequence that scores highest.

52
00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:10.850
The matrix becomes confident about something it has barely seen, and identifying homologues gets ambiguous.

53
00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:19.775
The same point, with the consensus sequence alongside — the sequence you get by taking a majority vote at each position of the alignment.

54
00:05:20.375 --> 00:05:26.525
Those counts become frequencies — each count divided by the number of sequences in the alignment.

55
00:05:27.125 --> 00:05:30.700
Read a column and you can see what the family tolerates.

56
00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:37.625
A column that is glycine in every sequence has a frequency of one for glycine and zero for everything else.

57
00:05:38.225 --> 00:05:46.600
A column with several residues spreads its frequency between them, and that spread is the information the profile adds over a single sequence.

58
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.825
Now the harder case: an alignment with gaps in it.

59
00:05:51.425 --> 00:05:54.225
Frequencies are not yet scores.

60
00:05:54.225 --> 00:06:05.275
A score has to say how much more often a residue appears here than it would by chance, and that is a ratio: the observed frequency divided by the background frequency of that residue.

61
00:06:05.875 --> 00:06:09.975
Take the logarithm of that ratio and you have a log-odds score.

62
00:06:09.975 --> 00:06:19.150
Positive means the residue is enriched at this position, negative means it is rarer than chance, and zero means the position says nothing about it.

63
00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:24.150
The matrix is filled in one position at a time, column by column.

64
00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:30.925
A conserved position produces one large positive score and a row of large negative ones.

65
00:06:31.525 --> 00:06:39.575
A variable position produces scores near zero across the board, which is the matrix saying that this column does not discriminate.

66
00:06:40.175 --> 00:06:47.775
And a position never observed with a given residue produces minus infinity, which is the problem the next slides fix.

67
00:06:48.375 --> 00:06:51.575
Minus infinity is too strong a claim.

68
00:06:51.575 --> 00:06:59.350
It says the residue is impossible here, when all the alignment really showed is that it was not seen in a handful of sequences.

69
00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:09.325
The remedy is a pseudocount: a small number added to every count before the frequencies are worked out, as though each residue had been seen a fraction of a time.

70
00:07:09.925 --> 00:07:17.150
With pseudocounts, no score is infinite, and the matrix generalises to sequences the alignment never contained.

71
00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:20.650
The size of the pseudocount matters.

72
00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:27.775
Too small and the profile is brittle; too large and it washes out the conservation that made the profile worth building.

73
00:07:28.375 --> 00:07:32.650
The third part of the lecture: profile hidden Markov models.

74
00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:42.925
A profile HMM is a statistical model of a family of sequences, built from a multiple alignment, which captures both the conserved parts and the variable ones.

75
00:07:42.925 --> 00:07:45.400
It has three kinds of state.

76
00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:50.875
Match states are the conserved positions, where particular residues turn up reliably.

77
00:07:50.875 --> 00:07:55.625
Insert states are extra residues that are not part of the conserved core.

78
00:07:55.625 --> 00:08:00.900
Delete states are positions where a sequence has nothing, where the core has something.

79
00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:02.475
Why hidden?

80
00:08:02.475 --> 00:08:05.675
Because you cannot read the state off the sequence.

81
00:08:05.675 --> 00:08:11.200
Given a residue, you cannot tell whether it belongs to a match state or an insert state.

82
00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:16.800
Given a gap, you cannot tell whether it is a genuine deletion or just a poor alignment.

83
00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:20.650
The states are inferred from probabilities, not observed.

84
00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:26.175
Match and insert states emit residues, with a probability for each of the twenty.

85
00:08:26.175 --> 00:08:30.275
Delete states emit nothing — that is what deleting means.

86
00:08:30.275 --> 00:08:32.925
And then there are the transitions.

87
00:08:32.925 --> 00:08:39.325
From a match state you can go to the next match state, to an insert state, or skip ahead to a delete state.

88
00:08:39.325 --> 00:08:44.925
From an insert state you can insert again — that is the self-loop — or return to matching.

89
00:08:44.925 --> 00:08:48.875
From a delete state you can keep deleting or rejoin.

90
00:08:48.875 --> 00:08:54.400
Those transitions are what let the model handle insertions and deletions of any length.

